      "sample_index": 0,
      "text": "Let the polynomial be $P(x) = x^3 + 3x^2 + ax + 7,$ and let $\\alpha$ be an integer root.  Then\n\\[P(\\alpha) = \\alpha^3 + 3\\alpha^2 + a\\alpha + 7 = 0.\\]Since $\\alpha$ is an integer, so is $3\\alpha^2 + 7,$ so $a$ must be of the form $q(3\\alpha^2 + 7),$ for some integer $q.$  Substituting this into the polynomial and rearranging:\n\\[ax^3 + bx^2 + cx + 7x^2 + 49x + 7 = (x-1)(x^2 + (3q+1)x + (3q+7)) = 0.\\]Since $(3q+1)$ and $(3q+7)$ are not both square-free, the roots of this quadratic are $\\frac{-3q-1 \\pm \\sqrt{9q^2 + 24q + 4}}{2}.$  Thus,\n\\begin{align*}\n\\frac{-3q-1 + \\sqrt{9q^2 + 24q + 4}}{2} &= \n\\begin{cases}\n\\alpha \\\\\n-\\alpha\n\\end{cases} \\\\\n\\frac{-3q-1 - \\sqrt{9q^2 + 24q + 4}}{2} &= \n\\begin{cases}\n\\alpha \\\\\n-\\alpha\n\\end{cases} \n\\end{align*}and $\\alpha$ is not $1$ or $-1.$",
