    {
      "sample_index": 2,
      "text": "If $a=x + 7$ for an integer $x,$ then $x$ must divide $37.$ Since the quadratic could potentially have integer roots, we may treat the cubic as a quadratic with integer coefficients by making the change\n\\[y = x + 1\\rightarrow \\rightarrow y^3 + 3y^2 + y(x+y-1) + 6 = (y-2)(y^2 + 4y + 3) = 0.\\]\nWith some easy factorization,\n$$(y-2)(y+1)(y+3) = 0 \\rightarrow \\rightarrow (y - 1)(y - 2)(y - 3)(y + 10) = 0$$\n$$\\rightarrow \\rightarrow (x - 1)^2(x - 2)^2(x - 3)^2 = 0,$$\nso here $x = 1, 2, -3.$ As we worked behind the scenes with sums by adding back in much easier variables, we have thus found\n\\begin{gather}\n1 + 17 = -16 \\\\\n2 + 17 = 19 \\\\\n-3 + 7 = 4.\n\\end{gather}\nThe only constants $a$ I found for this transformation, however, turning the cubic into a quadratic were $\\boxed{-16, 19, 4}$.\n\nMainly, let $37=a(x+7),$ then $x+7=\\displaystyle\\frac{37}{y}\\rightarrow \\rightarrow x=-7 + \\frac{37}{y}$ for an integer $y.$ Because the polynomial has integer coefficients, the value of $x+7$ must also be an integer. Last step, $ax+7a=\\mathrm{constant}$ has an $x$ term with a coefficient $a,$ and express $a$ in terms of $x,$ we can then see that the only rational numbers $x$ with rational coefficients are $x=\\pm 1, \\pm 2, ..., \\pm 10.$",
